EXTENSION "ArM5_The_Lion_and_the_Lily_ChampagneAndBurgundy_CeleresFollowerOfBonisagus" {
    NAME "Ars Magica Fifth Edition, The Lion and the Lily - Celeres, Follower of Bonisagus"
    VERSION "0.5.0"
    SPEC_VERSION "0.5"
    RELEASE_DATE "2026-10-06"
    DEPENDS_ON "ArM5_The_Lion_and_the_Lily_Core_Ext"

    # Celeres, Follower of Bonisagus (Chapter Nine: Champagne and Burgundy, p110): one character, its printed stat lines verbatim fields, its write-up the DESCRIPTION.

    #t87c3daf14859d17e6bbc40 ^"Celeres, Follower of Bonisagus" DEF {
        EXTENDS #tHoHTL05ExampleCharacterAA ^"Example Character"
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Role" STRING "Magus"
            ^"Covenant" LIST [#tdc4bd16737c7f4eef04a98 ^"Cunfin"]
            ^"Age" STRING "150? (Apparent age 100)"
            ^"Personality Traits" STRING "Forceful +3, Charming +2, Absent-minded +2"
            ^"Source" STRING "The Lion and the Lily"
            ^"Page" INTEGER 110
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Celeres’ life changed in near the end of the twelfth century when he discovered a copy of Chretien de Troyes’ *Perceval*. What was this “graal” that Perceval was seeking?\n\nWhat gave it such powers? Celeres’ perhaps overactive imagination was captured. He immediately set out to find as many books about King Arthur, the Knights of the Round Table, and especially the Grail, as he could. When he heard about the covenant of Cunfin, with its community of monks and its holy hermit, it seemed like something out of the romances he had been reading. Celeres made his way there at once and through sheer force of personality convinced Eloi to admit him as a member.\n\nCeleres is obsessed with the Grail. He is no longer concerned with finding it, as he knows he is not as young as he once was, and he has come to believe that the Grail is something that perhaps cannot actually be found. Still, he wishes to know as much about it as he can and to share that knowledge with his fellow magi. A true Bonisagus, he has begun composing a great story cycle about the Grail, which he hopes will enlighten others about its true, spiritual nature. His time at Cunfin has clearly influenced his views, as more than a few traces of Cistercian thought can be found in his manuscript.\n\nCeleres gets on quite well with the Redcaps that call Cunfin home, and he refers to them as his “knights.” He relies on them to bring him any news or word of the Grail that they encounter. He is also quite close to his last apprentice, Artorius, and he hopes that, if he is unable to finish his Grail cycle, Artorius will finish it for him."
    }

}
